 ## Overview
-This method applies procedural and texture-based color blending to surfaces extracted by the Transvoxel algorithm, solving the visual discontinuity of flat shading on dynamically changing isosurfaces. It differs from standard single-texture approaches by using triplanar mapping to avoid UV seams and blending between top/side textures based on surface normal orientation [S1, unofficial]. It also supports multi-material blending via texture arrays and vertex weights for complex terrain features like grass, stone, and forest patches [S2, unofficial].
+This method applies procedural and texture-based shading to the geometry generated by the Transvoxel algorithm, ensuring that material appearance (grass, stone, forest) blends smoothly across level-of-detail (LOD) boundaries and terrain slopes. It distinguishes itself from standard voxel texturing by combining triplanar mapping with a slope-aware material blending system that adapts to the local surface orientation, preventing texture stretching and maintaining visual continuity despite the irregular vertex distribution of the Transvoxel mesh.
 
